---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {datetimeSidebar} from '../../../data/datetime-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
import {dep} from '../../../data/site';
---

<DocsLayout title="Timezones on Kotlin/JS" sidebar={datetimeSidebar}
            description="Named time zones in the browser: no setup for Mp* types, optional data for raw kotlinx zones">

    <h1>Timezones on Kotlin/JS</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Named zones like <code>Europe/Berlin</code> work in the browser without any setup and without bundled timezone
        data.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing to set up</h2>

    <Code code={`val berlin = MpTimezone.of("Europe/Berlin")

MpInstant.now().atZone(berlin)                      // works in the browser as is
MpLocalDate.of(2022, 3, 27).atStartOfDay(berlin)`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>On JS, <code>ultra:datetime</code> reads zone offsets from the browser's own IANA database through
        <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code>, so it is as current as the browser and adds no data to your bundle. The zone
        math on top of it is shared with the JVM and Native and follows java.time:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>A local time in a DST gap moves forward by the length of the gap (02:30 on a spring-forward night becomes
            03:30).
        </li>
        <li>A local time in a DST overlap takes the earlier offset.</li>
        <li>Adding days or months keeps the time of day, and keeps the current offset if the result falls into an
            overlap.
        </li>
        <li>The start of a day whose midnight falls into a gap is the end of the gap.</li>
    </ul>

    <Aside>A test table generated from java.time runs on all three targets, and on the JVM the same algorithm is checked
        against java.time around every transition of every zone from 1900 to 2040.
    </Aside>

    <h2>Zone ids are case-sensitive</h2>

    <p>The browser's <code>Intl</code> accepts <code>europe/berlin</code>, java.time does not. So that an id accepted in
        the browser is also accepted on the server, <code>ultra:datetime</code> rejects case variants of known ids on JS
        too, with an <code>IllegalTimeZoneException</code>.</p>

    <h2>Raw kotlinx zones</h2>

    <p>Only code that calls <code>kotlinx-datetime</code> directly, e.g. <code>TimeZone.of("Europe/Berlin")</code>,
        needs timezone data in the browser. Without it kotlinx knows only UTC and fixed offsets. The
        <code>kraft:addons:datetime</code> module bundles a js-joda dataset for that case — call only the loader you
        need, so dead-code elimination keeps just that dataset:</p>

    <Code code={`dependencies {
    implementation("${dep.kraftAddonDatetime}")
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <div class="not-prose my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Loader</th>
                <th>Kraft builder</th>
                <th>Coverage</th>
                <th>Size (min / gzip)</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>installFullTimezones()</code></td>
                <td><code>fullTimezones()</code></td>
                <td>all zones, full history</td>
                <td>~713 / 37 KB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>installTimezones1970to2030()</code></td>
                <td><code>timezones1970to2030()</code></td>
                <td>years 1970&ndash;2030</td>
                <td>~130 / 18 KB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>installTimezones10YearRange()</code></td>
                <td><code>timezones10YearRange()</code></td>
                <td>~&plusmn;5 years from build</td>
                <td>~40 / 10 KB</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.kraft.addons.datetime.timezones1970to2030

val app = kraftApp {
    timezones1970to2030()   // only needed for raw kotlinx TimeZone.of(...)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Outside Kraft, call the matching <code>install&hellip;()</code> function once at startup, before the first zone is
        resolved.</p>

    <div class="not-prose mt-8 flex gap-4">
        <a href="/ultra/datetime/mp-timezone"
           class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
            MpTimezone &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
